Confirmed: only `e0793ff4c` touched the file between the introducing commit and the fix, and it didn't modify this section (verified above — it only appended new parameter-handling code after this block, unrelated to the bug).

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 0dd84a04f05c4a852194e92db650588d1a8166c0

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the full history of `SavedHqlExportToCSV.java` with `git log -p --follow`, looking for when the buggy `else` branch (the single-selection parameter handling that the fix wraps in `if (value != null)`) was first introduced. I confirmed no other commit touched that specific block between its introduction and the fix.

**(2) The introducing change:** Commit `0dd84a04f` ("HQL Reports: Export HQL", Mar 1 2017) added the loop:
```java
for (SavedHQL.Option option: SavedHQL.Option.values()) {
    if (!hql.getQuery().contains("%" + option.name() + "%")) continue;
    if (option.allowMultiSelection()) {
        ...
    } else {
        String value = helper.getParameter(option.name());
        SavedHQLInterface.IdValue v = new SavedHQLInterface.IdValue();
        v.setValue(option.name());
        Long id = option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value);
        ...
        v.setText(id == null ? "" : id.toString());
        params.add(v);
    }
}
```
For single-choice options (e.g. SUBJECT, DEPARTMENT), when the HTTP request did not include that parameter, `helper.getParameter(option.name())` returns `null`, but the code unconditionally called `option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), null)` and then added an `IdValue` with empty text to `params` regardless. This spuriously registers the option as "provided but empty," which breaks the CSV export logic downstream in `execute()` for single-choice parameters.

**(3) Evidence:** The fix commit `395d7ef9f` simply wraps this exact block in `if (value != null) { ... }`, guarding against the null case introduced in `0dd84a04f`. `git log` shows this block was untouched by any intervening commit (`e0793ff4c` only appended unrelated parameter-type handling code after it), so the introducing commit and the fixed code are directly matched with no confounding changes in between.

I'm confident in this identification — the fix is a minimal, targeted null-check reversal of exactly the code added in `0dd84a04f`, and no other commit modified that logic in the interim.

